menu "GBX systems"

choice GBX_SYSTEMS
    prompt "Systems in the firmware"
    default GBX_SYSTEMS_ALL
    help
        The systems the firmware and the test firmware run. A chip whose
        internal RAM holds the execution core of only one of them builds
        that one alone; the shared sdkconfig.defaults.<target> file makes
        the choice. The PC build has both.

config GBX_SYSTEMS_ALL
    bool "GB, GBC and GBA"

config GBX_SYSTEMS_GB_ONLY
    bool "GB and GBC only"
    help
        The GBA's core and JIT are not built. The GB's execution core
        takes their place in internal RAM, see tools/link_core_layout.py.

config GBX_SYSTEMS_GBA_ONLY
    bool "GBA only"
    help
        The menus list and load GBA games only, and the JIT's block in
        internal RAM is sized for the GBA's JIT alone. The GB's core stays
        in the build, in flash, which the app, the GBA's worker queues and
        the test firmware share.

endchoice

endmenu

menu "GBX internal RAM budget"

config GBX_GB_JIT_POOL_KB
    int "GB JIT: code pool in internal RAM, KiB"
    range 32 192
    default 192
    help
        The GB JIT's code pool, one contiguous block of internal RAM with
        16 KiB of shared code before it, set aside when the app starts. A
        board whose internal RAM cannot spare it all sets less in
        boards/<board>/sdkconfig.defaults; blocks the pool evicts run from
        the second level in PSRAM.

endmenu

menu "GBX PSRAM budgets"

config GBX_GB_JIT_STORE_KB
    int "GB JIT: second level of the code cache, KiB"
    range 256 16384
    default 8192
    help
        The copies of the GB JIT's ROM blocks that run from PSRAM after the
        code pool evicts them, one record per 256 bytes. Each board sets it
        for its PSRAM in boards/<board>/sdkconfig.defaults. With
        GBX_GB_JIT_STORE_LEAVE_KB, a game's second level takes at most this
        much; the test firmware takes it whole.

config GBX_GB_JIT_STORE_LEAVE_KB
    int "GB JIT: PSRAM the second level leaves free, KiB"
    range 0 4096
    default 0
    help
        With 0, the GB JIT's second level takes GBX_GB_JIT_STORE_KB when
        the JIT starts. Otherwise the game sets it up once the save and the
        instant saves have their memory: it takes the PSRAM left, up to
        GBX_GB_JIT_STORE_KB, less this much, which stays free for what the
        menus and the saves allocate while the game runs. A board whose
        PSRAM a large game fills sets it in
        boards/<board>/sdkconfig.defaults.

config GBX_GBA_ROM_CACHE_KB
    int "GBA: ROM cache, KiB"
    range 1024 32768
    default 14336
    help
        A GBA ROM up to this size stays whole in PSRAM, a larger one is
        paged in. It takes one contiguous block. Each board sets it for its
        PSRAM in boards/<board>/sdkconfig.defaults.

endmenu
